@Anonymous 

Agreed. Here’s an example from my own solve. I eventually got the “Jazz pianist Jamal” answer, even though I’ve never heard of him, after I had the last two crosses, so ___AD. I moved on, then came back to it when I was otherwise stuck. I figured Jamal is often an Arab first name, and Ahmad is often an Arab last name, so I penciled it in. Lo and behold, the newly-filled-in AHM quickly made their crosses apparent, confirming AHMAD for me while opening up the tricky north side of the puzzle. 

Knowing the trivia helps, but it’s also essential to be able to synthesize a lifetime of knowledge and vocabulary when the answer isn’t immediately attainable.

@Roldick 
I guess I just don't like a lot of proper names, whether I know them or not.

But, they are easy to look up, so this puzzle where i didn't know most of the names went 11 minutes faster than my average for Friday.

@Mean Old Lady 

It's still standing now, and appears to be in pristine condition. I used to live two blocks away from the Candler mansion, and I'd pass it every day on my way out of Candler Park and Little Five Points.

That entire neighborhood massively gentrified in the late sixties and early seventies. My wife and I moved into probably the last cheap apartments in the area over thirty years ago, and we were surrounded by the wealthy and powerful. The mayor lived a block from us.

